```css
/* Specificity: 0-0-1 (element) */
p { color: gray; }

/* 0-1-0 (class) — beats the element selector */
.note { color: navy; }

/* 0-1-1 (class + element) — beats .note alone */
.note p { color: darkgreen; }

/* 0-2-0 — two classes beat one */
.note.important { color: crimson; }

/* Last tie-breaker: same specificity → source order wins */
.note.important { color: firebrick; }
```

**Why this works:** When multiple declarations target the same element and property, the cascade first compares *origin and importance* (author styles beat user-agent defaults, `!important` flips the order), then *specificity*, then *source order*. Specificity is computed as a three-part count: IDs (1-0-0), classes/attributes/pseudo-classes (0-1-0), and elements/pseudo-elements (0-0-1) — the counts are compared column by column, so a single ID outranks any number of classes. Inline `style` attributes sit above all selector-based rules. In the example, `.note.important` (0-2-0) beats `.note p` (0-1-1) because the class column wins before the element column is even compared. Two rules with identical specificity resolve by whichever appears later — which is why putting overrides at the end of your stylesheet beats adding `!important`, and why `!important` sprinkled everywhere eventually makes a codebase unfixable.

```css
/* TRANSITION: state A → B, user-triggered */
.button {
  background: var(--accent);
  transition: background-color 0.25s ease-out, transform 0.25s ease-out;
}
.button:hover {
  background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--accent), black 15%);
  transform: translateY(-2px);
}

/* ANIMATION: multi-step, looping, runs on its own */
.pulse {
  animation: pulse 2s ease-in-out infinite;
}

@keyframes pulse {
  0%   { box-shadow: 0 0 0 0 color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 60%, transparent); }
  70%  { box-shadow: 0 0 0 12px transparent; }
  100% { box-shadow: 0 0 0 0 transparent; }
}
```

**Why this works:** A `transition` is a passive rule: it interpolates between two computed states when a property *changes* (via `:hover`, a class swap, or JS), and it is automatically reversible — moving the mouse out replays it backwards. Choose transitions for interactive feedback on known endpoints. An `@keyframes` `animation` is proactive: it runs when applied, supports any number of keyframes at arbitrary percentages, loops (`infinite`), can `alternate` direction, and has its own fill/delay/state properties (`animation-play-state`, `animation-fill-mode: forwards`) — choose animations for load sequences, loaders, and anything with more than two states or no user trigger. Performance rule for both: animate only `transform` and `opacity` (compositor-only, no layout/paint) and never animate layout properties like `width`, `top`, or `margin`; `box-shadow` in the keyframe above is acceptable for a rare looping effect but a large-scale `transform` ring is cheaper if it appears in hot paths.

