<style>
  /* Creates a NEW stacking context: z-index of .modal
     is trapped inside it. page has z-index auto, so the
     wrapper participates in page's context with level 1. */
  .page { transform: scale(1); }

  .modal-wrapper { position: absolute; z-index: 1; }
  .modal         { position: absolute; z-index: 99999; }

  /* Fix: give the wrapper a higher level than the header,
     or remove the transform that created the context. */
  .sticky-header { position: sticky; z-index: 2; }
</style>
```

**Why this works:** `z-index` only ranks elements *within the same stacking context* — it never fights across contexts. A new context is created by `position` with a `z-index` other than `auto`, but also by `transform`, `filter`, `opacity` below 1, `will-change`, `isolation: isolate`, `backdrop-filter`, `contain`, and more. Once `.page` has a transform, the modal's 99999 means nothing outside `.page`; the header compares only against the wrapper's effective level. Fixes, in order of preference: (1) remove the accidental context-creating property, (2) raise the wrapper's `z-index` above the competing element, (3) render the overlay at the document root instead of nesting it, or (4) use `isolation: isolate` deliberately to fence a subtree's stacking so its internal z-index values cannot leak out. `z-index: 99999` is a code smell — first find which context you are actually in (DevTools: Layout → stacking order) before raising numbers.

